# J49 — Setup target-facts honesty (keyless)  ⭐ release-blocking
#
# Keyless, deterministic. Setup's target-facts disclosure is filesystem/git-derived,
# so it needs no model key. Launch `--target <repo>` at a freshly-seeded git repo
# carrying one committed file plus one uncommitted (dirty) edit. Setup discloses the
# operator-designated path (never cwd), the `dirty` working-tree state, the
# `branch@revision`, and a detected-`languages:` row. With a dirty tree the facts
# must read `dirty`, never `clean`, and nothing is dressed up as a verified fact
# beyond the target binding the operator explicitly gave via `--target`.
#
# Caveat: macOS keychain / OAuth-cached credentials cannot be masked by env-unset,
# but this journey needs no credential — the facts are git/FS-derived.
name: Setup discloses honest git-derived target facts (dirty tree, path, languages)
size: 120x36
env-unset: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY OPENAI_API_KEY NO_COLOR
env: HOME=@TMP_HOME KUZUSHI_REDUCED_MOTION=1
# Seed a git repo with one committed file and then a dirty (uncommitted) edit so
# the working tree is genuinely dirty when Setup inspects it.
shell: R="@ART/j49-worktree-repo"; rm -rf "$R"; mkdir -p "$R"; git -C "$R" init -q -b main; printf 'import os\nos.system("ls")\n' > "$R/main.py"; git -C "$R" add -A; git -C "$R" -c user.email=devex@kuzushi -c user.name=kz-devex commit -qm seed >/dev/null 2>&1; printf 'print("uncommitted edit")\n' >> "$R/main.py"
launch: --target @ART/j49-worktree-repo
wait-for: 30 READY THE MAP RUN
# The candidate facts hydrate asynchronously with a generation stamp. The whole
# facts line renders only once the git/FS inspection resolves, so anchoring on
# the `dirty` working-tree token (a real disclosure word, no longer present in
# the fixture dir name) waits on the actual hydration instead of racing it.
wait-for: 20 dirty
snap: setup-target-facts
# The disclosed path is the operator-designated repo (the selected Target row
# wraps its full path), never a silent cwd substitution.
expect: j49-worktree-repo
# Honest working-tree state: a dirty tree reads `dirty`, never `clean`. This is
# the real target-facts token, not a substring of the fixture dir name.
expect: dirty
expect-not: clean
# Git revision line: `branch@revision`, derived and disclosed. Anchoring the
# revision proves the facts line hydrated, not a placeholder.
expect: main@
# Detected language is real, not the `detecting` placeholder: the seed file is
# Python, so the disclosure must name it.
expect: languages: python
ask: Setup discloses the target path, the `dirty` working-tree state, the branch@revision, and a detected-`languages:` row — are these the git/FS facts, with nothing claimed "confirmed" beyond the explicit `--target` binding, and no language/build guess dressed up as a verified fact?
